  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q3",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Differentiate $y=\\frac{x+1}{x-1}$ and show that the derivative is always negative.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^2}<0$ for all $x\\ne1$.\n\nThe squared denominator is positive on the domain.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{(x-1)-(x+1)}{(x-1)^2}\\\\ y'&=-\\frac{2}{(x-1)^2}\\end{align*}$$ For every $x\\ne1$, $(x-1)^2>0$. The derivative has a negative numerator and a positive denominator, so $y'<0$ throughout the domain.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 21
  },
